ick?", "s": 0.05, "e": 9.371}, {"text": "Around 240 BC, Eratosthenes heard that at noon on the summer solstice in southern Egypt, the sun shone straight down a deep well without casting a shadow.", "s": 9.551, "e": 21.654}, {"text": "Meanwhile, in Alexandria to the north, a vertical stick cast a 7.2-degree shadow.", "s": 21.774, "e": 28.376}, {"text": "Recognizing that 7.2 degrees is one-fiftieth of a circle, he multiplied the distance between the two cities by 50, nailing the Earth's circumference within roughly 1 percent accuracy.", "s": 28.496, "e": 42.377}]}